# Pull Request Behaviour

The PR — its title, linked issues, and labels — is the durable record of a
change. Change history lives in GitHub as a single lineage:

**GitHub issues → closing-keyword references in PRs → GitHub auto-generated release notes (from merged PRs).**

Use the `gh` CLI for all of the below (no hardcoded repo — `gh` infers it from
the current checkout).

## Merging is the developer's decision

Agents **must not merge pull requests.** Opening a PR, pushing more commits to
the feature branch, and updating the PR body are all fair game — but the
**merge itself is the developer's call**, made by a human after review. (This
may be revisited later; for now, state it plainly.)

### Always do

- Leave a PR **open** for the developer to review and merge once your work is
  pushed and the PR is up to date.
- Report that the PR is ready and hand the merge decision to the developer,
  linking the PR in the wrap-up so they can click straight through (see
  `human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`).
- Ask the developer to **let you know once it's merged**, so you can run the
  after-merge cleanup — see `git/git-branch-and-pr-workflow.mdc` ("After the
  developer merges the PR").

### Never do

- Run `gh pr merge` (or merge via the web UI, git, or any automation).
- Enable auto-merge, or otherwise cause the PR to merge without the developer
  explicitly deciding to.

## Creating issues

Don't **manufacture** a GitHub issue for routine work just to track it and
immediately close it. Adding a feature or making a change does **not** require
an issue — issue creation is optional (see `process.mdc`, where "GitHub issue
creation" is an optional intake step).

### Always do

- Track **genuine, pre-existing or standalone** work as an issue when it's
  worth tracking on its own.
- When an issue already exists, **reference and address it in the PR** and close
  it via the PR (see "Linking issues" below).

### Never do

- Create a throwaway issue for a change you're about to make in the same PR just
  to have something to close.

## Linking issues

Every PR that resolves an issue MUST reference it in the **PR body** with a
closing keyword, one issue per line:

```
Closes #12
Fixes #34
Resolves #56
```

- GitHub also accepts `Close`, `Fix`, and `Resolve`. Merging the PR then closes
  the linked issue automatically.
- Include a closing keyword for **each** issue the PR completes. A bare `#N`
  mention, `(#N)`, or prose does **not** auto-close — the keyword is required.
- Use `Refs #N` for issues that are related but should stay **open** after
  merge (context only, or a subset of acceptance criteria). Do not give such
  issues a closing keyword.
- Whenever a PR uses closing keywords, you MUST confirm GitHub actually
  registered the links after opening or editing the body:

```bash
gh pr view <number> --json closingIssuesReferences
```

  An empty array means **no** closing links were registered — a mistyped or
  misplaced `Closes` silently closes nothing. Fix the body and re-check until
  every issue you intend to close appears.

## Titles, labels & release-note lineage

- Write PR titles that are **clear, user-facing, and release-note-worthy** —
  merged PRs are exactly what GitHub's auto-generated release notes are built
  from, so the title is what users will read.
- If the repo uses labeled/categorized release-note generation (e.g.
  release-drafter or GitHub's category labels), apply the appropriate label to
  each PR so it lands in the right release-notes section.

## Test plan

Every PR you create or update MUST include a **Test plan** section in the PR
body written as **GitHub task-list checkboxes** (`- [ ]`) that enumerate the
manual verification steps a reviewer can follow. Never ship a PR with a
missing, empty, or throwaway ("it compiles", "n/a") test plan.

```
## Test plan

- [ ] Build / typecheck passes
- [ ] Automated tests pass
- [ ] <concrete manual step a reviewer can reproduce>
- [ ] <edge case or regression exercised by this change>
```

- Write **concrete, checkable, reproducible** items tied to the actual change —
  the exact steps to run and what a reviewer should observe — not vague filler.
- Cover automated checks **and** the manual verification a reviewer should run.
- Check items off (`- [x]`) as you verify them.
- When new commits change what needs testing, refresh these checkboxes as part
  of the push — see "Keep the PR in sync with the branch's commits" below.

Keep the checkbox Test plan above the `Closes #N` lines at the bottom of the
body.

## Managing the active PR

When the user asks you to "add" something to a PR (a feature, fix, change,
etc.), they mean the **currently active pull request** — the open PR for the
branch you are working on. Add the work to that PR; do **not** open a new one.

### Always do

- Identify the active PR before starting: `gh pr view` (current branch's PR) or
  `gh pr status`.
- Commit the new work to the **same branch** that PR tracks, then push so the
  existing PR updates:

```bash
git push   # pushes to the branch the active PR already tracks
```

- If you are on `main`/`master` or a branch with no open PR, ask the user which
  PR they mean instead of guessing or creating a new one.

### Never do

- Create a new branch + new PR for work the user asked to "add to the PR".
- Open a second PR that duplicates or splits off the active PR's work without
  being asked.

### Exception

Only open a new PR when the user **explicitly** asks for one (e.g. "open a
separate PR for this").

## Keep the PR in sync with the branch's commits

The PR body is the durable, human-readable **summary over the branch's
commits** (see `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`, "How CHANGELOG content reaches the
PR"). As commits accumulate on the branch, that summary goes stale unless you
refresh it — a body describing only the first commit misrepresents what will
merge and pollutes the release notes built from it.

### Always do

- Whenever you push new commits to an open PR, refresh the PR **body** in the
  **same push** — update the Summary and the Test plan (see "Test plan") so they
  reflect **all** commits now on the branch, not just the initial batch.
- Add Test-plan items for the new work and check off items as you verify them.
- Add `Closes #N` lines for any issues the new commits newly resolve, and
  re-check the registered closing links (see "Linking issues").
- Update the **title/labels** when the scope shifts, keeping the title
  release-note-worthy (see "Titles, labels & release-note lineage").

### Never do

- Push more commits while leaving the body describing only the first commit.
- Defer the body refresh to "later" — it is part of the same push that adds the
  work.
